course: everyday
lesson: 274
title: Helpers in town and building sites
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many people in town are there to help you: postal workers, doctors and nurses, police officers and firefighters — and they can help best when you know what to tell them.
explain: In a hospital emergency department the most serious cases are seen first, so a person with a small cut may wait while someone very ill goes ahead.
explain: A firefighter in a mask and breathing gear may look scary, but you never hide from them in a fire; you go towards them or call out, because they have come to save you.
explain: Building sites are only for trained workers in hard hats: machines, deep holes and falling things make them dangerous, and the driver of a big reversing machine cannot see a child.
explain: A girl in Austin tells the doctor, "It hurts here, on the right side of my tummy, since yesterday," and the doctor quickly knows where to look.
fact: You learn your postcode or ZIP code, because it helps letters to be delivered quickly.
fact: You tell the doctor clearly where it hurts, because it helps them find out what is wrong.
fact: In the emergency department the most serious cases are seen first; it is not first come, first served.
fact: A cast is kept dry, and you tell an adult if the arm hurts more, because the cast helps the bone heal.
fact: If you see a crime, you tell an adult or the police, because they can stop it.
fact: A firefighter in full gear may look scary but is there to help, and children should never hide from them in a fire.
fact: You never play with a fire hydrant, because it is for firefighters; opening one for fun wastes water and can leave firefighters without enough water pressure to fight a fire.
fact: When firefighters visit school, you ask questions and learn what to do in a fire.
fact: You never go inside the fence of a construction site, because machines, holes and falling things are dangerous.
fact: You walk around a hole in the road, because you could fall in.
fact: You stay well away from a big machine that is reversing, because the driver cannot see you.
fact: A hard hat area sign means stay out; only workers with protection may enter.
exercise: Why should you know your postcode or ZIP code? | It helps letters to be delivered quickly | It is the number of your house | It is your phone number | It tells you the weather
exercise: The doctor asks what is wrong. What do you do? | Tell clearly where it hurts and since when | Say "everything hurts" and then stay very quiet | Let the doctor guess where it hurts the most | Point at a different place so you do not get a shot
exercise: In the emergency department, who is seen first? | The most seriously ill or hurt people | The people who arrived first, in the order they came in | The people who shout the loudest | Children before adults
exercise: You waited a long time at the emergency department with a sprained ankle, while a man who came later went in first. Why? | His problem was more serious | He knew the doctor from before | He paid more money at the desk to go in first | He complained more loudly than you
exercise: You have a cast on your broken arm. What do you do? | Keep it dry and tell an adult if it hurts more | Take it off at night to rest | Get it wet to keep it cool on hot days | Scratch under it with a pencil
exercise: You see someone breaking into a car. What do you do? | Tell an adult or the police | Try to stop them all by yourself | Take a photo up close of their faces | Ignore it, because it is not your car
exercise: There is a fire and a firefighter in a big mask comes into your room. What do you do? | Go to them or call out, because they are there to help | Hide under the bed so they cannot find you | Hide in the wardrobe until they leave the house again | Run away from them
exercise: Why do some children hide from firefighters in a fire? | Firefighters in full gear can look scary | Firefighters tell children off for hiding | Children are told to hide from all adults | Firefighters only save grown-ups, not children
exercise: Your friend wants to open the fire hydrant on a hot day. What do you say? | No, it is for firefighters to use | Yes, it makes a great water park for us | Only if no grown-ups can see us playing | Only if we close it again quickly after
exercise: Firefighters visit your school. What is a good thing to do? | Ask questions and learn about fire safety | Hide behind the classroom door | Play with their hoses when they are not looking | Climb on the fire engine without asking
exercise: Your ball goes over the fence into a construction site. What do you do? | Leave it or ask a worker; never go inside the fence | Climb over quickly to get it before anyone notices | Squeeze under the fence | Go in after the workers go home
exercise: There is a hole in the road with a cone next to it. What do you do? | Walk around it, well away from the edge | Jump over it for fun | Look into it closely | Move the cone away
exercise: A digger is reversing on a building site. What do you do? | Stay well away, because the driver cannot see you | Wave so the driver sees you | Walk behind it to watch | Stand close to watch it work, as diggers move very slowly
exercise: What does a hard hat area sign mean? | Stay out; only workers with protection may enter | Children may come in if they wear a cap or a hood | Hats must be taken off here | A shop that sells hats
test: A boy in Leeds has a pain in his ear. Which helps the doctor most? | "My left ear hurts and it started this morning." | "I feel bad all over." | "I don't know, you tell me." | "My right ear hurts and it started this morning," when it is the left
test: Why is the emergency department not first come, first served? | People who are most seriously hurt need help first | The doctors like to choose | Nurses forget who came first | It is quicker to see children first, so they always go ahead of adults
test: A girl's cast gets soaked in the bath. Why is that a problem? | A wet cast can stop working and harm the skin | It will turn a different colour | It will make her arm heavier forever | Nothing; casts are waterproof, so she can bath and swim as she likes
test: In a smoky house a child hears a strange breathing sound and sees a big figure in a mask. What is most likely? | A firefighter coming to save them | A monster that came with the fire | A burglar using the smoke | A neighbour playing a game
test: Children in Philadelphia open a fire hydrant to play. What could go wrong? | Firefighters may not have enough water to fight a fire | The water will be too hot | The hydrant will play music | Nothing; hydrants are put there for children to play with in summer
test: Why should you stay away from big machines on a building site? | The drivers often cannot see children nearby | The machines are too noisy for your ears to bear for long | The machines are very slow | Workers like to be alone
test: A boy sees two people stealing a bike. What is the safest thing to do? | Tell an adult or call the police | Chase them on his own to get it back | Shout at them and try to grab the bike | Do nothing, because it is not his bike
test: You are writing a letter to your gran. Why must you put her postcode on it? | It helps the letter reach her quickly | It makes the stamp cheaper | It is the number of her phone | It shows how old she is
test: The fence round a building site has a gap. Why should you still not go in? | There are holes, machines and falling things | Builders will give you a job | The fence is only for decoration | You might find treasure buried in the ground, and it belongs to the builders
test: hurt my arm, been waiting ages in emergency, that guy just went before us?? | The most serious cases are seen first | It's always first come, first served | He paid more money | The doctor likes him better than you and your mum
test: digger reversing, can i stand close n watch | No, stay well away, the driver can't see you | Yes, it's going slowly | Yes, if you wave at the driver so he knows | Only behind it
test: there's a fire n a firefighter in a mask, i'm scared, hide under bed? | Never hide, go to the firefighter | Hide till they leave | Hide in the wardrobe | Lock the bedroom door so the smoke stays out
test: building site fence has a gap, cool stuff in there | Never go in, it's dangerous | Go in when no workers are there | Go in with a hard hat | Just peek in for a minute
explain: A boy in Colorado kept his cast in the bath because it felt itchy; the skin underneath got sore and infected, and the cast had to be cut off and done again.
explain: A girl in Dublin did not mention to the doctor that her tummy pain had moved to the lower right side; the next day it was much worse and it turned out to be appendicitis needing surgery.
fact: A wet cast can rub and infect the skin under it, so a cast must be kept dry.
fact: You never poke a pencil or anything else down a cast to scratch an itch, because it can scratch the skin underneath; you tell an adult instead.
fact: Doctors depend on what the patient tells them, so leaving out how a pain feels or where it moved can delay the right treatment.
test: cast is soooo itchy can i poke a pencil down it | No, it can scratch the skin; tell an adult | Yes, if the pencil is clean | Yes, a ruler is even better | Yes, then wash it out with cold water from the tap
test: tummy hurts but i dont wanna say where to the doctor its embarrasing | Tell the doctor exactly where and how it hurts | Just say you are fine | Say it hurts everywhere | Let the doctor guess by pressing all over until you jump
test: kids opened the fire hydrant to play in the water | It can lower water pressure firefighters need | It's fine in a heatwave | It's fine if no fire is nearby | It's fine if you close it later, once everyone has cooled down
test: my cousin says a boy hid in the wardrobe in a fire cos the firefighter looked like a monster, "they found him just in time". lesson? | Never hide from firefighters; they are there to help | Hide in a wardrobe until the fire goes out | Only come out if you know the firefighter by name | Firefighters only help grown-ups
test: did u hear a kid climbed in the building site and "fell in a hole 50 feet deep". what's prob exaggerated | The exact depth; the real lesson is stay out of building sites | That building sites have holes | That machines work on the site | That fences are there to keep people out
test: ppl say the new kid's dad "is in prison cos he robbed a bank" — nobody actually knows. spread it? | No, it's only a rumour and could really hurt them | Yes, it's interesting news | Yes, but say you only heard it | Yes, warn everyone in the class to stay away from him and his dad
exam: Identify the principle by which patients are attended to in an emergency department. | The most serious cases are attended to first | Patients are seen strictly in order of arrival | Children are invariably seen before adults | Patients who pay more are attended to first
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate response of a child to a firefighter in full protective equipment during a fire? | The child shall not hide but shall make themselves known | The child shall conceal themselves until the gear is removed | The child shall flee from the firefighter | The child shall respond to a familiar adult only
exam: It is given that a plaster cast has been applied to a fractured arm. Select the response consistent with its proper care. | The cast shall be kept dry and any increase in pain reported | The cast may be washed daily with warm water | The cast may be removed at night for comfort | The skin beneath shall be scratched with a pencil to relieve itching
exam: State the reason for which entry within the perimeter fence of a construction site is prohibited. | Machinery, excavations and falling objects are dangerous | The site is the private garden of the workers | Entry merely delays the completion of the works by the builders | The materials stored there are of great value
